Use the canonical multiplicative equivalence \(G \simeq^\times \mathrm{ConjAct}\,G\) as the organizing mechanism.

First, obtain the cardinality equality from its underlying type equivalence. For the biconditional, prove both sides independently: the right side is exactly preservation of multiplication by this `MulEquiv`; the left side follows by set extensionality, unfolding membership in the conjugation orbit and in the carrier of the conjugacy class. Both memberships say that an element is a conjugate \(hgh^{-1}\) of \(g\), up to the harmless choice of conjugation orientation. Thus the useful intermediate goals are:

- identify `toConjAct (x * y)` with `toConjAct x * toConjAct y`;
- show \(x\in\operatorname{orbit}(g)\iff x\) is conjugate to \(g\);
- transfer finite cardinality along the underlying equivalence.

The group assumption is essential only for the orbit/class identification, since conjugation uses inverses; the cardinality and multiplication statements need only the type synonym and its multiplicative equivalence.

